<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newport, Rhode Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Newport is a city on Aquidneck Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> Bay, Rhode Island. Founded in 1639 by settlers including William Coddington and John Clarke, it became one of the leading ports of colonial America, prospering from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>, including the slave trade, and was home to an early Jewish community with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touro-synagogue">Touro Synagogue</a>. In the 19th century it became a summer resort for wealthy Americans, known for its mansions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island.jpg" alt="Newport, Rhode Island" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MVASCO · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newport_Rhode_Island_Aerial_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Newport lies on Aquidneck Island in Narragansett Bay, about 33 miles south-east of Providence and 74 miles south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. It is the most populous municipality on the island. The Newport Bridge, the longest suspension bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, joins it to Conanicut Island across the East Passage of the bay. The city has a total area of 11.4 square miles, of which about a third is water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Eight founders, among them Nicholas Easton, William Coddington and John Clarke, began the settlement on the island's southern side in 1639. Many had belonged to the settlement at Portsmouth with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-hutchinson">Anne Hutchinson</a> and her followers. Newport grew to be the largest of the four original settlements that formed the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations, and in 1640 the second Baptist congregation in Rhode Island was formed there under John Clarke.</p><p>Newport was a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in colonial and early America. Ships carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and molasses produced by enslaved people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> to Rhode Island, where it was distilled into rum and exchanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> for captives. In 1764 Rhode Island had about 30 rum distilleries, 22 of them in Newport. Captives continued to be brought in illegally after a state law of 1787 and federal acts of 1794, 1800 and 1807. On 8 December 1776 a British force occupied the city without opposition, which closed a haven for privateers on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><p>From the middle of the 19th century wealthy families built summer houses, beginning with Kingscote in 1839 and Chateau-sur-Mer in 1852, and between 1870 and 1915 large mansions modelled on the royal palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Newport Historic District, one of three National Historic Landmark Districts in the city, has one of the highest concentrations of colonial houses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Mansions such as The Breakers, Marble House and Rosecliff are open as museums. The city has been linked to the Navy for generations: it held the Naval Academy during the Civil War, and it houses the Naval War College. It staged the first American national tennis championships in 1881, the first U.S. Open golf tournament in 1895 and the America's Cup races from 1930 to 1983. Its population was 25,163 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Newport, Rhode Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jeshuat Israel congregation dedicated the Touro Synagogue in <b>Newport, Rhode Island</b>, designed by Peter Harrison and built from 1759 under the leadership of Isaac Touro.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Anne Hutchinson's followers founded Portsmouth in 1638, a group split off to found <b>Newport</b> in 1639, and Samuel Gorton founded Warwick in 1642.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a></li></ul></section>
